  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a constitution?",
      "a": "A constitution is a country's top set of rules. It says how the government is organised, what powers each part has and what rights people have. Other laws must fit with it.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a constitution do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What rights are in a constitution?",
      "a": "Many constitutions protect free speech, equality before the law, freedom of religion, fair trials and voting. Some, like South Africa's, also include rights to housing, water and education.",
      "asks": [
        "What are fundamental rights?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can a constitution be changed?",
      "a": "Yes, but it is usually harder than changing an ordinary law. The USA needs two-thirds of Congress and three-quarters of the states; India needs a special majority in parliament; Ireland and Australia need a public vote.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an amendment?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who checks that laws follow the constitution?",
      "a": "Usually a top court, such as India's Supreme Court, the US Supreme Court or Germany's Federal Constitutional Court. It can cancel laws that break the constitution.",
      "asks": [
        "Can a court cancel a law?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does every country have a constitution?",
      "a": "Nearly every country has one. Most are a single written document, but the UK, New Zealand and Israel have no single document; their rules are spread across laws and customs.",
      "asks": [
        "Does the UK have a constitution?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The US Constitution, written in 1787, is the oldest written national constitution still in use.",
    "India's constitution came into force on 26 January 1950, celebrated as Republic Day."
  ],
